---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHero from '../../components/PageHero.astro';

const prompt = `Act as my 3D reconstruction coach. I want to understand how a captured room becomes an editable scene. Explain the roles of measured geometry, photographs, camera positions, an agent harness and a visual comparison loop. Give me a three-step plan using the public 3D Model Anything office example. For every observation, distinguish what is visible in the viewer from what cannot be checked without the original scans. Do not invent measurements. Finish with a review checklist.`;
---
<BaseLayout title="Turn a room into an interactive 3D model" description="Explore a real interactive room, study its source and use an agent to practice a scan-to-3D workflow.">
  <PageHero compact eyebrow="3D · Real-world showcase" title="Turn a room into a world" accent="." subtitle="Explore a real 3D office. Then work backward through the scan, agent and evidence that made it." ctas={[
    { label: 'Explore the finished room', href: 'https://wesbos.github.io/3d-model-anything/', icon: 'arrow-right' },
    { label: 'Start the kata', href: '#start', variant: 'ghost', icon: 'play' },
  ]} />

  <section class="section outcome" aria-labelledby="outcome-title"><div class="container">
    <div class="outcome-grid">
      <div><p class="eyebrow">01 / See the outcome</p><h2 id="outcome-title">Walk around a real 3D office</h2>
        <p>Wes Bos built an editable Three.js office from a room capture with an AI coding agent. Open his viewer and try the dollhouse, wireframe and walk-around views.</p>
        <p><a class="primary" href="https://wesbos.github.io/3d-model-anything/">Open the interactive room ↗</a></p>
        <p class="note">This is Wes Bos’s public example, not a Dojo-created reconstruction. The published office does not include its original scans or photos, so scan and overlay modes are unavailable for that example.</p>
      </div>
      <figure><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JGQCcCzuS88" aria-label="Watch Wes Bos explain 3D Model Anything on YouTube"><img src="/images/wes-bos-3d-video-thumb.png" width="1920" height="1080" alt="" loading="lazy" /></a><figcaption>Watch the creator’s video on YouTube. Image: Wes Bos, used with his project under its <a href="https://github.com/wesbos/3d-model-anything/blob/31b2dc9da3fb541e501f2f0936e55b32afb70ee8/ASSETS-LICENSE.md">asset terms</a>.</figcaption></figure>
    </div>
  </div></section>

  <section class="section" id="start" aria-labelledby="start-title"><div class="container narrow">
    <p class="eyebrow">02 / Try it now</p><h2 id="start-title">Start with the device you have</h2>
    <ol><li>Open the finished room. Choose two views and notice what each reveals.</li><li>Copy the prompt below into an AI assistant on your phone or computer. Ask it to help you understand the workflow.</li><li>Check its explanation against the <a href="https://github.com/wesbos/3d-model-anything">creator’s repository</a> and <a href="https://wesbos.com/3d-model-anything">walkthrough</a>. Correct anything unsupported.</li></ol>
    <label for="room-prompt">Your prompt</label><textarea id="room-prompt" rows="8" readonly>{prompt}</textarea>
    <div class="copy"><button type="button" data-copy hidden>Copy prompt</button><span data-copy-status aria-live="polite">Or select and copy the text.</span></div>
    <p class="note">The public example is enough for this first exercise. You do not need to scan your home, install a plugin or create an account to explore the viewer.</p>
  </div></section>

  <section class="section ingredients" aria-labelledby="ingredients-title"><div class="container narrow">
    <p class="eyebrow">03 / Assemble the stack</p><h2 id="ingredients-title">What makes it work?</h2>
    <dl>
      <div><dt>Outcome</dt><dd>An editable Three.js room and browser viewer—not a certified building model.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Harness and model</dt><dd>The creator describes using a coding agent; use the assistant and model available to you for the study exercise. For your own reconstruction, choose a compatible coding harness and check its file and command permissions.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Instructions and context</dt><dd>A clear reconstruction brief, capture geometry, reference photos and recorded camera positions. Do not invent missing dimensions.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Skill and tools</dt><dd>The repository includes the community-created <code>scan-to-threejs</code> skill, viewer and helper scripts. Its README names Node.js 22.12+, Python 3 and additional dependencies for particular comparisons. A local skill is optional for studying the example and is not installed by this page.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Verification</dt><dd>Compare rendered views against available evidence; record uncertainty and manually check measurements. The public office lacks original reference assets, so it cannot demonstrate the private scan-to-photo comparison itself.</dd></div>
    </dl>
    <p>Before running an outside repository or enabling its skill, inspect its instructions, scripts, dependencies and requested file/network access. Run it with only the permissions and data you intend to share. Account limits and model usage charges vary.</p>
  </div></section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="adapt-title"><div class="container narrow">
    <p class="eyebrow">04 / Adapt and verify</p><h2 id="adapt-title">Make the technique yours</h2>
    <details><summary>Phone or browser: study the example</summary><p>Use the viewer, video and prompt above. Write down one visible detail, one inference and one thing you cannot verify without the source scan. Ask your assistant to revise any unsupported claim.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Computer: inspect and run the public viewer</summary><p>Read the <a href="https://github.com/wesbos/3d-model-anything#run-the-viewer">official setup and asset terms</a> first. With Git and a supported Node.js release installed, clone the repository, enter it, install its dependencies and start its development server. These commands download and execute third-party software; inspect the repository and lockfile before running them.</p><pre><code>git clone https://github.com/wesbos/3d-model-anything.git
cd 3d-model-anything
npm ci
npm run dev</code></pre><p>Follow the repository’s displayed local address. Check the room in more than one view and save an observation of what you can and cannot verify. This is the creator’s workflow; Dojo has not run it for you.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Optional: capture a room you have permission to scan</summary><p>The source project describes a LiDAR-equipped iPhone or iPad for new scans and Xcode to build its app from source. Check the <a href="https://github.com/wesbos/3d-model-anything/tree/main/apps/house-capture">current app instructions and device requirements</a>; do not assume an app-store release. Follow its repository instructions, review the skill and helpers, and keep captures private. Images and spatial data can reveal addresses, possessions or other people’s information.</p></details>
    <h3>Quick check</h3><details><summary>Does an attractive 3D room prove its measurements are correct?</summary><p>No. Compare against the source measurements and photographs where available, document uncertainty and have the result independently reviewed for its intended use.</p></details>
    <p>For BIM, a picture or mesh alone does not establish IFC objects, properties, relationships or compliance. Construction, safety and professional decisions need independent validation and any required approval.</p>
    <p><a href="/katas/inspect-ifc">Inspect a real BIM information model</a> · <a href="/katas">Explore more katas</a> · <a href="/katas/industry-workshops">Industry workshops</a> · <a href="https://github.com/wesbos/3d-model-anything">Explore the project on GitHub</a></p>
  </div></section>
</BaseLayout>
<script>
  const button = document.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-copy]');
  const input = document.querySelector<HTMLTextAreaElement>('#room-prompt');
  const status = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-copy-status]');
  if (button && input && status) {
    button.hidden = false;
    button.addEventListener('click', async () => {
      try { await navigator.clipboard.writeText(input.value); status.textContent = 'Copied. Paste it into your assistant.'; }
      catch { input.focus(); input.select(); status.textContent = 'Text selected. Use your device’s copy command.'; }
    });
  }
</script>
<style>
  .narrow { max-width: 68rem; }
  .eyebrow { color: var(--rust-text); font-weight: 800; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .08em; }
  .outcome, .ingredients { background: var(--paper-raised); }
  .outcome-grid { display: grid; gap: var(--space-5); grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); align-items: center; }
  @media (min-width: 60rem) { .outcome-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; } }
  figure { margin: 0; } figure img { display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; border-radius: var(--radius); } figcaption, .note { font-size: var(--text-sm); color: var(--ink-muted); }
  .primary, .copy button { display: inline-block; background: var(--gold); color: var(--gold-ink); padding: .75rem 1rem; border: 1px solid var(--gold); border-radius: var(--radius); font: inherit; font-weight: 800; text-decoration: none; }
  .copy { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; align-items: center; gap: var(--space-3); margin-top: var(--space-3); } .copy button { cursor: pointer; } .copy button[hidden] { display: none; }
  label { display: block; font-weight: 700; margin-block: var(--space-4) var(--space-2); } textarea { width: 100%; max-width: 70ch; padding: var(--space-4); font: inherit; color: var(--ink); background: var(--paper); border: 1px solid var(--line-strong); border-radius: var(--radius); }
  dl { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 17rem), 1fr)); gap: var(--space-3); } dl div { padding: var(--space-4); background: var(--paper); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: var(--radius); } dt { font-weight: 800; } dd { margin: .4em 0 0; }
  details { padding: var(--space-3) 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); } summary { cursor: pointer; font-weight: 800; } pre { overflow-wrap: anywhere; white-space: pre-wrap; padding: var(--space-3); background: var(--paper-sunk); border-radius: var(--radius); } li + li { margin-top: var(--space-2); }
</style>
